  "notes": "Reyes Rios + Larrain's Casa Sisal, a single-storey house within the historic Hacienda Sac Chich near Acanceh (the article's photographs, no plan): flat concrete roofs on deep overhangs over cream chukum-plastered walls, floor-to-ceiling sliding glass that disappears into the structure. The formal front: a lily pond with steps down, glimpsed through wide-open glass into a lit room at night. West of it, a lower wing holds the sunken living room and kitchen, opening onto its own terrace with a small stepped water feature and a freestanding outdoor shower/pergola post (guessed from the pergola patio described but not fully shown). A long lawn, given the two-acre former henequen field, runs south between two rows of Royal Palms (the night photograph's all\u00e9e) to a linear pool at the far end (the pool most clearly photographed; the described three-sided infinity pool on the opposite side is guessed and not separately modelled). The hacienda's 11-foot perimeter walls are suggested at east and west only, to keep the lawn open in the view. Guessed throughout: the exact plan, the roof step for the cantilevered exterior stair (simplified to three landings), the second pool's shape. No dimensions were published; scaled from the photographs against door heights (~2.1 m)."
